      "question": "What is the survival rate of invasive ductal carcinoma?",
      "type": "ai_overview",
      "text_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "snippet": "The survival rate of invasive ductal carcinoma (IDC), the most common type of breast cancer, varies significantly based on the stage of the cancer at diagnosis. When diagnosed and treated early, the five-year survival rate is nearly 100%. However, if the cancer has spread to other parts of the body (metastatic disease), the five-year survival rate drops considerably.",
          "snippet_highlighted_words": [
            "When diagnosed and treated early, the five-year survival rate is nearly 100%"
          ],
          "reference_indexes": [
            3,
            5
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "snippet": "Here's a more detailed breakdown:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "list": [
            {
              "snippet": "Localized (cancer confined to the breast): The five-year survival rate is approximately 99%.",
              "reference_indexes": [
                0,
                1,
                4
              ]
            },
            {
              "snippet": "Regional (cancer spread to nearby lymph nodes): The five-year survival rate is around 86%.",
              "reference_indexes": [
                0,
                2
              ]
            },
            {
              "snippet": "Distant (cancer spread to distant parts of the body): The five-year survival rate is around 32%.",
              "reference_indexes": [
                0,
                2
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "snippet": "It's important to remember that these are just general statistics and individual survival rates can vary based on several factors, including:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "list": [
            {
              "snippet": "Stage of the cancer: Earlier stages generally have better survival rates.",
              "reference_indexes": [
                1,
                3
              ]
            },
            {
              "snippet": "Tumor grade: Higher grade tumors (more aggressive) may have lower survival rates.",
              "reference_indexes": [
                9,
                13
              ]
            },
            {
              "snippet": "Hormone receptor status: Hormone receptor-positive tumors often have a better prognosis.",
              "reference_indexes": [
